window.docContents[128].resolve({"/Connectivity/#section-euler-s-theorem":{"contents":"Both cases of Euler's theorem now follow. Let q be a longest trail from a to b\n(trails have length at most G.edgeSet.ncard, so one exists as long as there is any\ntrail q₀ from a to b). If q is not Eulerian then\nIsTrail.exists_longer_of_not_isEulerian gives a longer trail, a contradiction. The\ntwo cases differ only in why deleting the edges of a trail leaves every degree even,\nso we take this as the hypothesis hdel.\n\nvariable [G.LocallyFinite] [DecidableEq V] [Finite G.edgeSet]\n\nlemma exists_isEulerian_of_isTrail {a b} (hG : G.EdgesInOneComponent)\n    (ha : a ∈ G.support) {q₀ : G.Walk a b} (hq₀ : q₀.IsTrail)\n    (hdel : ∀ q : G.Walk a b, q.IsTrail →\n      ∀ x, Even ((G.deleteEdges {e | e ∈ q.edges}).degree x)) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk a b, p.IsEulerian := by\n  classical\n  -- P k says there is a trail from a to b of length k\n  let P : ℕ → Prop := fun k => ∃ q : G.Walk a b, q.IsTrail ∧ q.length = k\n  -- let q be a longest trail from a to b\n  obtain ⟨q, hq, hq_len⟩ := Nat.findGreatest_spec (P := P) (m := q₀.length)\n    (n := G.edgeSet.ncard) hq₀.length_le_ncard_edgeSet ⟨q₀, hq₀, rfl⟩\n  refine ⟨q, by_contra fun hne => ?_⟩\n  -- the graph with q's edges deleted has even degrees, so q can be extended\n  obtain ⟨q', hq', hlt⟩ := hq.exists_longer_of_not_isEulerian hG ha (hdel q hq) hne\n  have : q'.length ≤ Nat.findGreatest P G.edgeSet.ncard :=\n    Nat.le_findGreatest hq'.length_le_ncard_edgeSet ⟨q', hq', rfl⟩\n  grind\n\n\nEuler's theorem (existence direction, closed case): a graph with finitely many\nedges, all in one component (EdgesInOneComponent), in which every vertex has even\ndegree has a closed Eulerian walk. [Nonempty V] is needed only so that we can name\na start vertex in the case that the graph has no edges. In that case the walk is\nnil, which is not a circuit (IsCircuit requires p ≠ nil), so we don't call it\nan Eulerian circuit.\n\nEuler's theorem, closed case: a graph with finitely many edges, all in one\ncomponent, in which every vertex has even degree has a closed Eulerian walk./-- **Euler's theorem**, closed case: a graph with finitely many edges, all in one\ncomponent, in which every vertex has even degree has a closed Eulerian walk. -/\ntheorem exists_closed_isEulerian [Nonempty V]\n    (h : G.EdgesInOneComponent) (h2 : ∀ v, Even (G.degree v)) :\n    ∃ u : V, ∃ (p : G.Walk u u), p.IsEulerian := by\n  by_cases hsupp : G.support.Nonempty\n  · -- pick a non-isolated start u; a longest closed trail from u is Eulerian\n    obtain ⟨u, hu⟩ := hsupp\n    exact ⟨u, exists_isEulerian_of_isTrail h hu .nil\n      fun q hq => hq.even_degree_deleteEdges_closed h2⟩\n  · -- no non-isolated vertex: G is edgeless, so nil is Eulerian\n    obtain ⟨u⟩ := ‹Nonempty V›\n    exact ⟨u, nil,\n      (isEulerian_nil_iff u).mpr (Set.not_nonempty_iff_eq_empty.mp hsupp)⟩\n\n\nIf G has at least one edge then the closed Eulerian walk uses it, so it is not\nnil and is an Eulerian circuit: in Mathlib this is IsEulerian together with\nIsCircuit.\n\nEuler's theorem, circuit form: a graph with at least one edge, finitely many\nedges, all in one component, and every degree even has an Eulerian circuit./-- **Euler's theorem**, circuit form: a graph with at least one edge, finitely many\nedges, all in one component, and every degree even has an Eulerian circuit. -/\ntheorem exists_isEulerian_isCircuit (h : G.EdgesInOneComponent)\n    (h2 : ∀ v, Even (G.degree v)) (hE : G.edgeSet.Nonempty) :\n    ∃ u : V, ∃ p : G.Walk u u, p.IsEulerian ∧ p.IsCircuit := by\n  obtain ⟨x, y, hxy⟩ := Sym2.exists.mp hE\n  have : Nonempty V := ⟨x⟩\n  obtain ⟨u, p, hp⟩ := exists_closed_isEulerian h h2\n  -- p uses the edge s(x, y), so it is not nil\n  refine ⟨u, p, hp, hp.isTrail, ?_⟩\n  rintro rfl\n  simpa using hp.mem_edges_iff.mpr hxy\n\n\nEuler's theorem (existence direction, closed case) for a connected graph:\nfollows from exists_closed_isEulerian via Connected.edgesInOneComponent.\n\nEuler's theorem, closed case, for a connected graph: if every vertex has even\ndegree then there is a closed Eulerian walk./-- **Euler's theorem**, closed case, for a connected graph: if every vertex has even\ndegree then there is a closed Eulerian walk. -/\ntheorem Connected.exists_closed_isEulerian (h : G.Connected)\n    (h2 : ∀ v, Even (G.degree v)) :\n    ∃ u : V, ∃ (p : G.Walk u u), p.IsEulerian :=\n  have : Nonempty V := h.nonempty\n  SimpleGraph.exists_closed_isEulerian h.edgesInOneComponent h2\n\n\nEuler's theorem (existence direction, open trail): if G has all edges in one\ncomponent (EdgesInOneComponent) and a ≠ b are the only two odd-degree vertices,\nthen G has an Eulerian trail from a to b.\n\nEuler's theorem, open case: if the edges of G all lie in one component and\na ≠ b are its only vertices of odd degree, then G has an Eulerian trail from a\nto b./-- **Euler's theorem**, open case: if the edges of G all lie in one component and\na ≠ b are its only vertices of odd degree, then G has an Eulerian trail from a\nto b. -/\ntheorem exists_isEulerian_trail_of_two_odd {a b}\n    (hG : G.EdgesInOneComponent) (hab : a ≠ b)\n    (hpar : ∀ x, Even (G.degree x) ↔ x ≠ a ∧ x ≠ b) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk a b, p.IsEulerian := by\n  -- the two odd-degree vertices a, b are non-isolated, hence in G.support\n  have hsupp : a ∈ G.support ∧ b ∈ G.support := by\n    simp only [← degree_pos_iff_mem_support]; grind [Odd.pos]\n  -- since a and b lie in the same component there is a path, hence a trail\n  obtain ⟨q₀, hq₀⟩ := (hG a hsupp.1 b hsupp.2).some.toPath\n  exact exists_isEulerian_of_isTrail hG hsupp.1 hq₀.isTrail\n    fun q hq => hq.even_degree_deleteEdges_open hab hpar\n\n\nEuler's theorem (existence direction, open trail) for a connected graph:\nfollows from exists_isEulerian_trail_of_two_odd via\nConnected.edgesInOneComponent.\n\nEuler's theorem, open case, for a connected graph: if a ≠ b are its only\nvertices of odd degree, then there is an Eulerian trail from a to b./-- **Euler's theorem**, open case, for a connected graph: if a ≠ b are its only\nvertices of odd degree, then there is an Eulerian trail from a to b. -/\ntheorem Connected.exists_isEulerian_trail {a b} (h : G.Connected)\n    (hab : a ≠ b) (hpar : ∀ x, Even (G.degree x) ↔ x ≠ a ∧ x ≠ b) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk a b, p.IsEulerian :=\n  exists_isEulerian_trail_of_two_odd h.edgesInOneComponent hab hpar\n\n\nEuler's theorem, full characterization: a finite graph with at least one vertex has\nan Eulerian trail iff all its edges lie in one component and it has either no\nodd-degree vertex or exactly two. The forward direction is\nIsEulerian.edgesInOneComponent with Mathlib's IsEulerian.card_odd_degree; the\nreverse direction is exists_closed_isEulerian (no odd-degree vertex) or\nexists_isEulerian_trail_of_two_odd (exactly two). We only reason about the number\nof odd-degree vertices, so we count them with Set.ncard and [Finite V] suffices;\nit implies the section's [Finite G.edgeSet] assumption, which we omit. Mathlib's\nIsEulerian.card_odd_degree counts using Fintype.card instead, so the forward\ndirection converts between the two.\n\nomit [Finite G.edgeSet]\n\n\nEuler's theorem: a finite graph with at least one vertex has an Eulerian\ntrail iff its edges all lie in one component and it has 0 or 2 vertices of odd\ndegree./-- **Euler's theorem**: a finite graph with at least one vertex has an Eulerian\ntrail iff its edges all lie in one component and it has 0 or 2 vertices of odd\ndegree. -/\ntheorem exists_isEulerian_iff [Finite V] [Nonempty V] :\n    (∃ u v, ∃ p : G.Walk u v, p.IsEulerian) ↔\n      G.EdgesInOneComponent ∧ ({v | Odd (G.degree v)}.ncard = 0\n        ∨ {v | Odd (G.degree v)}.ncard = 2) := by\n  have := Fintype.ofFinite V\n  refine ⟨fun ⟨_, _, _, hp⟩ => ⟨hp.edgesInOneComponent, by\n    classical\n    rw [← Nat.card_coe_set_eq, Nat.card_eq_fintype_card]\n    convert hp.card_odd_degree⟩, ?_⟩\n  rintro ⟨hG, h0 | h2⟩\n  · -- no odd-degree vertex: every degree is even, so there is a closed Eulerian walk\n    rw [Set.ncard_eq_zero, Set.eq_empty_iff_forall_notMem] at h0\n    obtain ⟨u, p, hp⟩ := exists_closed_isEulerian hG fun x =>\n      Nat.not_odd_iff_even.mp (h0 x)\n    exact ⟨u, u, p, hp⟩\n  · -- exactly two odd-degree vertices a, b: an Eulerian trail from a to b\n    rw [Set.ncard_eq_two] at h2\n    obtain ⟨a, b, hab, hS⟩ := h2\n    have hpar (x) : Even (G.degree x) ↔ x ≠ a ∧ x ≠ b := by\n      simpa [← Nat.not_odd_iff_even] using (Set.ext_iff.mp hS x).not\n    exact ⟨a, b, exists_isEulerian_trail_of_two_odd hG hab hpar⟩\n\nend EulerExistence\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Eulerian Circuits and Trails","header":"10.3.2. Euler's Theorem","id":"/Connectivity/#section-euler-s-theorem"},"/Constructions/#section-path-graphs":{"contents":"In Mathlib the canonical path graph on n vertices is constructed as the Hasse\ndiagram of the linear order on Fin n, so we first describe Hasse diagrams.\n\nGiven a poset (P, ≤) we say that a ∈ P is covered by b ∈ P if a < b and\n∀ c ∈ P, a < c → ¬c < b. In Mathlib this is denoted a ⋖ b.\n\nThe Hasse diagram of a poset (P, ≤) is the graph with vertex set P and edges\nbetween pairs of vertices such that one is covered by the other.\n\nThe Hasse diagram of the divisors poset of 60\n\nexample {P : Type*} {u v : P} [PartialOrder P] :\n    (hasse P).Adj u v ↔ u ⋖ v ∨ v ⋖ u := by rfl\n\n\nThe path graph on n vertices is then the Hasse diagram of Fin n. Recall that\naddition in Fin n is defined mod n.\n\nexample : (2 : Fin 4) + 2 = 0 := rfl\n\n\nSo when describing adjacency in this graph we need to use the underlying ℕ values.\n\n#source pathGraph\n\n\nexample (n : ℕ) (a b : Fin n) :\n    (pathGraph n).Adj a b ↔ a.val + 1 = b.val ∨ b.val + 1 = a.val := pathGraph_adj\n\n\nWe introduce notation for the path graph on Fin n.\n\nP[n] is the path graph pathGraph n on Fin n./-- P[n] is the path graph pathGraph n on Fin n. -/\nscoped notation \"P[\" n \"]\" => pathGraph n\n\n\nThe path graphs P[2], P[4] and P[7]\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Basic Families of Graphs","header":"3.1.3. Path Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-path-graphs"},"/Distance/#section-finite-eccentricity":{"contents":"The degree hypothesis of the Moore bound only makes sense when every vertex has\nfinitely many neighbours, that is, when G is locally finite, and this is the\nonly finiteness assumption the Moore bound needs. Every vertex at distance at most\ni + 1 from r is either at distance at most i or adjacent to such a vertex, so\nby induction each ball around r in a locally finite graph is finite.\n\nlemma finite_setOf_edist_le [G.LocallyFinite] (r : V) (i : ℕ) :\n    {v | G.edist r v ≤ i}.Finite := by\n  induction i with\n  | zero => simp\n  | succ i ih =>\n    refine (ih.union (ih.biUnion fun u _ => (G.neighborSet u).toFinite)).subset ?_\n    intro v (hv : G.edist r v ≤ ↑(i + 1))\n    rcases hv.lt_or_eq with hlt | heq\n    · push_cast at hlt\n      exact Or.inl (Order.le_of_lt_add_one hlt)\n    · obtain ⟨u, huv, hu⟩ := exists_adj_edist_eq i (by exact_mod_cast heq)\n      exact Or.inr (Set.mem_biUnion (x := u) (by simp [hu]) huv)\n\n\nIf r has eccentricity at most k then every vertex lies in the ball of radius k\naround r, so a locally finite graph with a vertex of finite eccentricity is finite.\n\ntheorem finite_of_eccent_le [G.LocallyFinite] {r : V} {k : ℕ} (h : G.eccent r ≤ k) :\n    Finite V :=\n  Set.finite_univ_iff.mp\n    ((finite_setOf_edist_le r k).subset fun _ _ => edist_le_eccent.trans h)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Moore and Cage Bounds*","header":"11.5.2. Finite Eccentricity","id":"/Distance/#section-finite-eccentricity"}});